text:
Setup for some coming examples

define a mini version of the NHANES data:


minidata &lt;- nhanes |&gt;
  slice(1:7) |&gt;
  select(seqn, ridageyr, bpxosy1, bpxodi1)

Insisting on two-dimensional output

tidyverse users would do this instead:


minidata |&gt; summarize(mean = mean(bpxosy1, na.rm = TRUE))
#|   mean
#| 1  124


the result is a data frame, not a single number:


minidata |&gt; 
  summarize(mean(bpxosy1, na.rm = TRUE)) |&gt;
  class()
#| [1] "data.frame"

Labeling categorical variables

using factor():


nhanes &lt;- nhanes |&gt;
  mutate(country = factor(
    dmdborn4,
    levels = c(1, 2),
    labels = c('USA', 'Other')
  ))


or, using base_match() from my baseverse package:


nhanes &lt;- nhanes |&gt;
  mutate(country = base_match(dmdborn4, 'USA' = 1, 'Other' = 2))

Categorizing continuous variables

using cut():


nhanes &lt;- nhanes |&gt;
  mutate(cholesterol = cut(
    lbxtc,
    breaks = c(0, 200, 240, Inf),
    labels = c('Desirable', 'Borderline high', 'High'),
    right = FALSE
  ))

Example: a data snippet

with piping, I could instead write this:


nhanes |&gt;
  subset(smoking == 'History of smoking',
         select = c(hypertension, smoking)) |&gt;
  subset() |&gt;
  head()


notice that this allows me to avoid nested functions

Example: a data snippet

I can split the use of subset() into two uses to make it even easier to read:


nhanes |&gt;
  subset(smoking == 'History of smoking') |&gt;
  subset(select = c('hypertension', 'smoking')) |&gt;
  head()

Pipelines

a cooking analogy:


cookie_dough |&gt;
  chill(hours=24) |&gt;
  scoop(ounces=1.75, quantity = 20) |&gt;
  roll() |&gt;
  bake(minutes=14)


each pipe adds to the result up to that point
for example, the third line calls for scooping the chilled dough

Keep writing comments

if you want to write long pipelines, I suggest writing comments within the pipeline, like this:


nhanes &lt;- nhanes |&gt; 

  # subset to people born outside of the us
  subset(country=='Other') |&gt;

  # define age in months
  transform(months=ridageyr*12) |&gt; 

  # define height in feet  
  transform(height_feet=bmxht*0.3937008/12)

Defining missing values

the replace() strategy for defining missing values from Session 6 of A Slower Introduction to R can be combined with transform() and piping:


nhanes &lt;- nhanes |&gt; 
  transform(
    ltpa_vigorous = replace(pad820, pad820 == 7777 | pad820 == 9999, NA)
  )

Labeling categorical variables

using factor():


nhanes &lt;- nhanes |&gt;
  transform(country = factor(
    dmdborn4,
    levels = c(1, 2),
    labels = c('USA', 'Other')
  ))


or, using base_match() from my baseverse package:


nhanes &lt;- nhanes |&gt;
  transform(country = base_match(dmdborn4, 'USA' = 1, 'Other' = 2))

